    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Ecomorphology links morphological adaptation to ecological function. The direct-developing Neotropical frog clade Terrarana includes multiple speciose genera occupying a numerous ecologies with specific microhabitat affiliations (Pedial et al. 2014, AmphibiaWeb 2016). They inhabit Central and South America, where they have been able to cover a wide geographic and elevational range in part due to their unique development and life history. However much of their preferred moist subtropical and tropical forests have been subject to deforestation and land use change (Redo et al. 2012), leading to the extinction and endangerment of many species ill-equipped to survive in segmented forests and open pastures (IUCN 2008). I investigated the relationship between morphology and ecology of species within three genera of the clade Terrarana: Craugastor, Eleutherodactylus, and Pristimantis to understand how and why certain species struggle to survive in disturbed habitats while close relatives thrive. I aimed to answer the questions: What morphological features best sort between ecologies? Are species of certain ecologies more successful in invading disturbed habitats? What differences in morphology do disturbance-tolerant frogs possess? I characterized species by microhabitat and generalized environment, and found that species hind limb length best sorted genera and environments, but their forelimb length best sorted between microhabitats. Arboreal frogs were most common in disturbed landscapes, along with species of the genus Eleutherodacytlus. Using computational approaches, I found evidence of a similar pattern of filtering for smaller body size in disturbed habitats across genera and microhabitats. These results suggest that exaptations have predisposed some species as disturbance-tolerant, while other species may have been filtered out of some habitats in part due to morphology.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
